# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for the GPU tests and the FFmpeg tests (used by the thread-sanitizer
# preset only). A race: entry suppresses a report when the stack of either racing access (or of the
# allocation of the memory) has a frame inside the library, so a race between dvv code and the
# library is hidden as well; races between two accesses in dvv code are still reported.
#
# Vulkan drivers (here Mesa lavapipe) are not instrumented: they synchronize their own objects with
# primitives ThreadSanitizer cannot see (futex-based locks), so concurrent, valid calls such as
# creating and destroying buffers from several threads are reported as races between the driver's
# malloc and free.
race:libvulkan_lvp.so
# FFmpeg's shared libraries are not instrumented: libavcodec's slice and frame threads synchronize
# their own buffers with primitives ThreadSanitizer cannot see (atomics and progress flags in the
# uninstrumented code), so the only accesses it sees from them are the intercepted calls (memset,
# memcpy, malloc and free inside libavcodec and libavutil), and valid decoding is reported as races
# between FFmpeg's threads and with the dvv code that reads a decoded frame after FFmpeg has handed
# it over. called_from_lib: ignores the memory accesses of every intercepted call made from code
# inside the named library (matched against the path of the loaded file, e.g. libavcodec.so.62);
# unlike race:, it keeps the instrumented code that FFmpeg's threads run checked (today the dvv log
# callback). Same list as dvv-components/tests/tsan.supp.
called_from_lib:libavutil.so
called_from_lib:libavcodec.so
called_from_lib:libavformat.so
called_from_lib:libswscale.so
called_from_lib:libswresample.so
called_from_lib:libavfilter.so
